      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{4x+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4x+1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{4y+1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{4y+1} \\\\ x(4y+1) & =3 \\\\ 4xy+x & =3 \\\\ 4xy & =3-x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3-x}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3-x}{4x}.\n$$",
